# MCP Skeleton

@../../packages/mcp-architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md

This MCP keeps the architecture every Python MCP in this repo shares. Read
[`packages/mcp-architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../../packages/mcp-architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md)
before you write code here: ruff, basedpyright and import-linter check most of it.

Its [Commands](../../packages/mcp-architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md#commands) install, check and
serve this MCP, with `mcp-skeleton` for `<name>`.

## This MCP

{{ options.description }}

{% if options.upstream == "openapi" %}
- Upstream: OpenAPI, from the vendor's spec in `upstream/openapi.yml`. `upstream/routes.py` lists the operations served as tools straight from the spec, and `operation-ids` in `[tool.mcp-openapi]` the ones our tools call through `Upstream`.
{% elif options.upstream == "custom" %}
- Upstream: A client of our own in `upstream/client.py`, started from the scaffold's HTTP client: one `request` for any method and path, retried by `RetrySettings`, parsing every body into a pydantic model. The `new-mcp` skill's rules for coding a client apply.
{% else %}
- Upstream: None. `upstream/` stays empty until the MCP calls a system of its own; the `new-mcp` skill's rules for coding a client say how to add one.
{% endif %}
{% if not options.stores %}
- Persistence: None.
{% else %}
- Persistence:
{%- if "postgres" in options.stores %} Postgres through SQLAlchemy, as `Persistence.database`. Models subclass `persistence.Base`; `uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m '...'` writes a migration, which the chart's migration hook applies.{% endif %}
{%- if "redis" in options.stores %} Redis as a key-value store (`py-key-value-aio`), as `Persistence.store`.{% endif %}

{% endif %}
{% if options.auth %}
- Auth: Each user signs in at `/sign-in` with their own credentials for the upstream. `shared/contracts/auth.py` is what the layers agree on: the `AuthStore` protocol and the `UpstreamCredentials` each call carries. `mcp/auth/` is the OAuth server the MCP's clients use; `persistence/auth/` implements the store, the credentials encrypted with `MCP_AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY`; `upstream/credentials.py` says how a request is signed with them, and `verify_credentials` checks a sign-in at `UPSTREAM_VERIFY_PATH`. `Upstream` signs every request with the credentials of the user the call acts for, so contexts never see them. [`guides/auth.md`](../../packages/mcp-architecture/guides/auth.md) says how to change what is kept or sent.
{% else %}
- Auth: Not implemented. `run_server` refuses to serve beyond loopback until the server has an auth provider, `FastMCP(auth=...)`. The `new-mcp` skill's rules for coding auth say how to add one.
{% endif %}
